<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Slovene translation.

    English, in `values/`, is the source language (SPEC §9); this file is a
    translation of it and is kept complete. The rules are the ones that hold
    everywhere: positional placeholders, <plurals> for anything that agrees in
    number, and the `name` attributes left untouched.

    The reader is addressed with **vikanje**, and that is Android's own habit
    rather than a house style. The system strings extracted from this phone's
    `framework-res.apk` and `Settings.apk` share the two registers **by
    context**, not by weight of numbers: a preference title, a button and a
    switch take the bare imperative (Izberi datoteko, Namesti, Izklopi,
    Nastavi datum), while the body of a dialog and an error message take the
    vy form (Dotaknite se, Preverite, Poskusite, Izberite, Vnesite). This
    file follows the same split: a control takes the bare imperative — Prekliči,
    Izbriši, Nadaljuj, Preskoči, Poskusi znova, Osveži — and a sentence takes
    the vy form. A confirmation dialog asks „Želite …?“, which is what
    Settings does (`dev_logpersist_clear_warning_title`).

    Typography. Quotation marks are **„ … “**. An **ellipsis stands off its
    word by a space** — „Iskanje …“, „Nalaganje …“ — which is what 90 of the
    93 system strings holding one do; here that space is a non-breaking one
    (U+00A0), so the three dots cannot be left alone at the head of a line.
    The dash that breaks a sentence is **–** with a space on either side, not
    the em dash the English file uses; that is the one piece of punctuation
    changed inside a format-only string, `city_label` (`%1$s – %2$s`). The
    apostrophe, where a network's name carries one (Vélib’, Vélo’v), is **’**
    (U+2019) and never the straight quote, which is why nothing here is
    escaped.

    **Slovene counts to four, and its `two` is a real dual.** One bike is
    „kolo“, two are „kolesi“, three or four are „kolesa“ and five are „koles“:
    four genuinely different words, and no <plurals> below repeats one form in
    two categories — except the three `freshness_*` ones, where „pred“ governs
    the instrumental and the instrumental plural of a noun is the same at three
    as at five. That duplication is the language, not a line left unfinished;
    see docs/glossary/sl.md.

    Slovene declines, and a case is a suffix. A sentence built around a
    placeholder has to stay right whatever the placeholder holds, so several
    lines here are turned around rather than given an ending they could not
    control: a name is set in apposition after a noun that carries the case
    („do postaje %1$s“), or the line becomes a label with a colon.

    A **preposition** in front of a placeholder is the same trap read from the
    other end: „z“ becomes „s“ before p, t, k, s, š, c, č, f and h, and the
    word that follows a search placeholder is whatever the reader typed. The
    three „no match“ lines therefore carry no preposition at all — they lean
    on „ustrezati“, which governs the dative, and the dative falls on
    „iskanju“, a word this file chose and can decline.

    See docs/glossary/sl.md for the words this file holds to, and
    CONTRIBUTING.md to submit a translation.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <string name="app_name">Roue Libre</string>

    <!-- Main screen: the station list. -->
    <string name="stations_title">Postaje</string>

    <!-- The number of bikes available to borrow at a station.
         kolo · kolesi · kolesa · koles — the dual is the second form and is
         not the plural. -->
    <plurals name="bikes_available">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d kolo</item>
        <item quantity="two">%1$d kolesi</item>
        <item quantity="few">%1$d kolesa</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d koles</item>
    </plurals>

    <!-- The number of free docks to return a bike to. A free dock is a
         "prosto mesto"; the dock counted as capacity is a "stojalo"
         (`docks_total`), which is the pair of words the screen needs when it
         shows both figures side by side. -->
    <plurals name="docks_available">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d prosto mesto</item>
        <item quantity="two">%1$d prosti mesti</item>
        <item quantity="few">%1$d prosta mesta</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d prostih mest</item>
    </plurals>

    <!-- Spoken by screen readers on a station's indicator.
         Example: "Trubarjeva cesta, 6 koles, 26 prostih mest". -->
    <string name="station_content_description">%1$s, %2$s, %3$s</string>
    <!-- Android's own wording, `settings:radioInfo_service_out`. -->
    <string name="station_out_of_service">Ne deluje</string>
    <string name="station_out_of_service_since">%1$s · zadnji podatek %2$s</string>
    <string name="station_availability_unknown">Razpoložljivost ni znana</string>

    <!-- What a station is called when its feed publishes no name for it and no
         street to stand in for one. -->
    <string name="station_unnamed">Neimenovana postaja</string>

    <!-- Shown for a station the network publishes outside the area its data
         covers. The short form joins the rest of the row, so it stays
         lowercase. -->
    <string name="station_beyond_area_short">zunaj zemljevida</string>
    <string name="station_beyond_area">Ta postaja je zunaj območja, ki ga pokrivajo nameščeni podatki. Njena kolesa se štejejo v realnem času, do nje pa ne sežeta ne zemljevid ne izračun poti.</string>

